<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ CONCEPT NOTE ══ -->
<div class="review-note">
  <div class="icon">📋</div>
  <div>
    <strong>Phase 13 Concept Note — For Phase 14 Refinement</strong>
    <p>
      Phase 13 is the validation boundary: nothing in this system should be committed to prototype fabrication 
      until all 12 bench tests are complete. The rhombic drives are the highest-value addition — they eliminate 
      the piston side-thrust problem (which was the primary long-term wear concern for the only two pistons in the system) 
      and simultaneously add 3,600W of generation. The air brake system is the most safety-critical new addition: 
      all four calipers must pass T-5 fade and ABS tests before the vehicle is operated by any person. 
      The 20% conservative margin is the discipline that separates this from earlier phases — every figure in the 
      budget is a pessimistic real-world estimate, not a theoretical peak. If bench results beat the margin, 
      Phase 14 gets an unexpected surplus. If they meet it exactly, the system still works. 
      Phase 14 should focus on: (1) full prototype vehicle build using Phase 13 bench-validated specs, 
      (2) low-speed track testing (0–30 mph) with instrumented logging of all PCW sensor channels, 
      (3) brake certification testing per FMVSS 135 equivalent, (4) first endurance run of 100 miles, 
      (5) water consumption measurement to establish real-world top-up requirement per 100 miles. 
      The MHD sleeve lab validation result (T-12) determines whether it enters Phase 14 scope. 
      All Phase 11 dynamo concepts (A, B, C) should be built and bench-compared in parallel with T-1 through T-6 
      to identify the most practical idle bootstrap for the prototype.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

